Subject: [PATCH 12/12] Read the ASR delivery's source once, front to back

Reason: On 2026-10-30 a desktop task with separation off sat in the vocal
stage for what would have been hours, on a 71-minute 48 kHz mp3, with the
task log silent after `encoding ASR vocal delivery without separation`.
`stream_asr_frames` reopened its input and seeked to every window. That input
is the separator's flac normally, but with `--no-separate` it is the source
itself, and libsndfile seeks an mp3 by decoding from the start -- so each
window cost time proportional to its offset and the encode was quadratic in
the length. The window made it worse: it was a fixed 2000 ratio steps, which
is 20 s at 44.1 kHz but 0.125 s at 48 kHz, so 34000 of those reads.

The input is now opened once and read sequentially, the context a window
needs from its neighbour kept from the previous read. The window and its
context are sized in seconds (20 s and 0.2 s, rounded up to whole steps),
which keeps exactly the old step counts at 44.1 kHz. The samples written do
not change: tests compare against a whole-file resample, and the 44.1 kHz and
48 kHz outputs were byte-identical to the old code's on mp3 input. A 30-minute
48 kHz mp3 now encodes in about 3 s. Cloud-shared; it changes only how the
input is read.

Removal: Delete once upstream streams the ASR delivery without seeking.

